==================================================
Lab 8: SQL Injection Attack - Querying the Database
Type and Version on MySQL and Microsoft
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab is almost identical in purpose to lab 7, finding out what database software is running behind the
scenes using a UNION attack, except this time the target database is MySQL or Microsoft SQL Server instead of
Oracle. Different databases provide this information in different ways, so the exact syntax used here is
different from the Oracle version, even though the underlying idea is exactly the same.

Goal: use a UNION attack to make the database reveal its own version string, directly on the product page.

==============================
WHY THE SYNTAX DIFFERS FROM ORACLE

In lab 7, we had to select from a special Oracle-only view called v$version, and every SELECT statement needed
to explicitly name a table, even a placeholder one like dual. MySQL and Microsoft SQL Server work differently.
Both of them support a special built-in system variable, written as two at symbols followed by the word version,
which directly holds the version string without needing to be selected from any table at all. This is simpler
than the Oracle approach, since there is no FROM clause required whatsoever.

Also, instead of using two dashes as a comment, this lab’s payload uses a single hash symbol instead. Both MySQL
and some other databases support the hash symbol as a way to comment out the rest of a line, functioning the
exact same way as the double dash trick used everywhere else.

=============================
STEP 1: CONFIRM COLUMN COUNT AND TEXT COMPATIBILITY

Exactly like every previous UNION attack lab, we start by confirming the query returns two columns, both of
which accept text, using two simple placeholder values wrapped in quotes. This step does not change at all
between different database types, since it is really just testing the structure of the original query, not
anything specific to MySQL or Oracle.

=============================
STEP 2: QUERY THE VERSION VARIABLE

Instead of selecting from a named view like in Oracle, we simply select the built-in version variable directly,
placing it into the first column, and leaving the second column as NULL since only one piece of information is
needed. Because this variable does not come from any real table, no FROM clause is required at all, the database
already knows what it refers to automatically.

======================================
WHAT THE PAYLOAD LOOKS LIKE

The category value sent is: a single quote, then UNION SELECT, then two at symbols followed by the word version,
comma, NULL, then a hash symbol at the end to comment out anything remaining in the original query.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and go to a product category page, intercepting the request with Burp Suite.
Confirm the query returns two text-compatible columns, using a UNION SELECT with two placeholder text values.
Replace the placeholders with the built-in version variable in the first column and NULL in the second, ending
the payload with a hash symbol instead of two dashes.
Send the request, and observe the database version string displayed directly on the page.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library for sending web requests, and the re library for searching text using patterns.

It asks for the category filter address.

It builds the first payload, used purely to confirm column count and text compatibility, consisting of a single
quote, UNION SELECT, two placeholder text values separated by a comma, and a hash symbol at the end. This is sent
as the category parameter, and a short snippet of the response is printed, so both placeholder values can be
visually confirmed as showing up correctly without error.

It then builds the real version-leaking payload: a single quote, UNION SELECT, the built-in version variable,
comma, NULL, and a hash symbol. This is sent the same way, as the category parameter.

The response HTML is cleaned the same way as earlier labs, removing script and style blocks first, since these
can contain unrelated version-shaped text such as file paths or library references, then converting all
remaining tags into newlines, then removing blank lines, leaving only genuinely visible text to search through.

A pattern is used to search for anything shaped like a version number, specifically digits, a dot, more digits,
another dot, and more digits, optionally followed by extra characters like a dash and more version details. Every
cleaned line matching this shape is printed, so the real database version can be identified among the results,
even if more than one line happens to match the pattern.

If nothing matches at all, every cleaned line is printed instead, so the actual response can still be reviewed
manually to locate the version string by eye.

=============================
WHY WE STRIP SCRIPT AND STYLE TAGS AND CLEAN THE HTML FIRST

An earlier attempt at this script searched the entire raw, unprocessed response text for anything containing a
period, which accidentally matched all kinds of irrelevant things, such as file paths and stylesheet references
that happened to contain dots as well, like resource file names. This produced incorrect, misleading results that
looked like a version number but were not. Cleaning the HTML first, removing script and style blocks and
converting tags into newlines, ensures the search is only performed over genuinely visible page content, which
is a much more reliable place to find the real injected version string.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is the same as every other lab, user input reaches a SQL query without being checked or
cleaned. The specific technique shown here, querying a built-in system variable instead of a named view, simply
reflects how MySQL and Microsoft SQL Server choose to expose this particular piece of information differently
from how Oracle does it.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so user input can never be interpreted as SQL structure, including attempts to read
built-in system variables.

Other good practices:

Restrict database account permissions where possible, even though built-in system variables like this one are
often readable by any account without special permission, so this particular piece of information may be
difficult to fully hide.
Keep database software updated, since an attacker who learns the exact version may also be able to look up known
vulnerabilities specific to that version.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

Different databases expose their version information in different ways, Oracle through a special view requiring
a FROM clause, MySQL and Microsoft SQL Server through a simple built-in variable requiring no FROM clause at all.
The hash symbol works as a comment character in MySQL, serving the same purpose as the double dash used in other
databases.
When searching a page’s response for leaked data, cleaning the HTML first and searching only visible text is far
more reliable than searching the raw, unprocessed response, since raw HTML often contains misleading, unrelated
text that can look like real data at first glance.
Despite the differences in exact syntax, the core technique remains a standard UNION attack, built on the same
column-counting and text-compatibility checks used throughout every other UNION based lab.
